WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
In traditional Android development, you build user interfaces

2
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:09.000
by writing layout XML and bringing it to life using a layout inflator.

3
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:13.000
This compilation process generates the R.layout classes,

4
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:16.000
hard-coding your UI structure into the native build.

5
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:20.000
React Native does not use this XML inflation pipeline.

6
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:24.000
There is no layout inflator and no R.layout generation.

7
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:29.000
For many native developers, this absence of XML triggers an assumption.

8
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:35.000
If the framework isn't inflating layouts, it must be painting web elements onto a hidden canvas.

9
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:40.000
That assumption is incorrect. React Native does not render to a web view.

10
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:43.000
It produces real native Android widgets on the screen.

11
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:47.000
Developing for React Native means removing the XML middleman

12
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:50.000
and interacting with native pixels through code.

13
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:53.000
Historically, React Native communicated with the native layer

14
00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:55.000
through an asynchronous JSON bridge,

15
00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:58.000
serializing and deserializing every layout command,

16
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:01.000
which created a performance bottleneck.

17
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:05.000
That bridge has been replaced by direct synchronous communication.

18
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:08.000
This is powered by Fabric, the new C++ renderer,

19
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:11.000
and the JavaScript interface, or JSI,

20
00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:15.000
which acts as the connective tissue between the two environments.

21
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.000
Now, when you write a JSX-slash-view tag,

22
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:21.000
it traverses this synchronous JSI channel

23
00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:26.000
and Fabric allocates an actual Android.view.view in memory.

24
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:29.000
While the programming model shifts from imperative to declarative,

25
00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:31.000
the target remains the same.

26
00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:34.000
You are still manipulating an Android view tree,

27
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:38.000
making your existing widget knowledge a direct map for the new syntax.

28
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:41.000
To map this out, we start at the root container of your application,

29
00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:44.000
the base level that holds every other element.

30
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:47.000
In Android, you choose from specific structural containers,

31
00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:50.000
such as linear layout for sequential stacking,

32
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:53.000
or constraint layout for relative positioning.

33
00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:56.000
React Native replaces these diverse view groups

34
00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:58.000
with one generic component, the view.

35
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:00.000
Functionally acting like a frame layout,

36
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:03.000
this component defaults to a vertical column direction,

37
00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:06.000
serving the same role as a vertical linear linear out.

38
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:09.000
Rather than defining relational coordinates per widget,

39
00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:13.000
all spatial logic is handled by the centralized flexbox engine,

40
00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:15.000
which calculates the internal spacing and positioning.

41
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:17.000
There is one structural constraint.

42
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:20.000
A view cannot contain raw string data.

43
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:24.000
Passing raw text directly into a view will throw a runtime exception.

44
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:27.000
By reducing the container model to a single component,

45
00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:31.000
structural complexity is moved out of the view type and into the layout engine.

46
00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:35.000
To render strings on screen, the data must be wrapped inside a text component,

47
00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:37.000
which is the replacement for Android's text view.

48
00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:41.000
Instead of XML attributes, you apply props to control behavior,

49
00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:43.000
such as number of lines for truncation,

50
00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:47.000
or adjust font size to fit to scale typography to its container.

51
00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:51.000
For user data entry, Android's edit text maps to the text input component.

52
00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:55.000
React Native inputs operate as controlled components.

53
00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:59.000
The input field does not hold its own internal text state.

54
00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:02.000
It is a reflection of the application state held in memory.

55
00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:04.000
Like an Android text watcher,

56
00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:07.000
typing triggers the on-change text event.

57
00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:10.000
This event updates the state, which then loops back

58
00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:12.000
to drive the value displayed in the text box.

59
00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:16.000
Input rendering requires treating the component as a visual output

60
00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:19.000
of your current memory data, not an independent data holder.

61
00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:23.000
Older React Native touch handlers, like touchability opacity,

62
00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:26.000
have been superseded by a unified API.

63
00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:30.000
The pressable component is now the replacement for standard Android widgets

64
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:32.000
like button or image button.

65
00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:37.000
Pressable utilizes hitslop, which serves as the functional equivalent to Android's touch delegate.

66
00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:41.000
Hitslop expands the interactive target area for accessibility,

67
00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:45.000
without altering the visual calculations of the layout engine.

68
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:49.000
React Native consolidates touch event math into a single wrapper component,

69
00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:52.000
simplifying how interactive layers are applied.

70
00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:55.000
Rendering static views requires minimal overhead,

71
00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:59.000
but dynamic infinite data lists require active memory management.

72
00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:02.000
The scroll view component is a non virtualized tool.

73
00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:04.000
It mounts all of its children at once,

74
00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:07.000
so it is designed for short finite content.

75
00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:09.000
Feeding large dynamic data sets into a scroll view

76
00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:12.000
will cause high memory usage and eventual crashes.

77
00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:15.000
For long lists, you must use the flat list component,

78
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:18.000
the structural equivalent to Android's recycler view.

79
00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:20.000
Flat list relies on view recycling.

80
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:23.000
Items scrolling outside the viewport are unmounted,

81
00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:26.000
and their resources are repurposed for new items entering the screen.

82
00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:31.000
This table shows how the native Android adapter pattern maps to react native props.

83
00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:35.000
The sizing function of adapter.get item count translates to passing an array

84
00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:37.000
into the data prop and reading its length.

85
00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:42.000
The UI construction handled by adapter.on bind view holder

86
00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:46.000
is mapped to a render item function that returns your JSX.

87
00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:51.000
The list diffing logic of diffutil.item callback is handled by the key extractor prop,

88
00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:55.000
letting reacts reconciliation engine identify changes.

89
00:04:55.000 --> 00:05:00.000
Complex list virtualization relies on the same memory recycling mechanics as native Android,

90
00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:05.000
executed through declarative syntax rather than Java or Kotlin adapters.

91
00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:10.000
Beyond the component tree, you must account for the physical constraints of modern mobile hardware.

92
00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:15.000
Device screens are interrupted by system status bars, navigation overlays,

93
00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:17.000
and physical camera punch holes.

94
00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:21.000
By default, react native will render UI elements directly into these physical zones,

95
00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:23.000
causing data to be obscured.

96
00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:26.000
To fix this, use safe area view.

97
00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:30.000
It calculates system insets and snaps your views into the readable zones.

98
00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:34.000
A reliable interface design anticipates the physical hardware topography of the device,

99
00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:37.000
not just the logical pixel grid.

100
00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:43.000
The structural building blocks of a react native interface map to the native Android widgets you already know.

101
00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:50.000
Transitioning to this framework is an exercise in remapping your vocabulary to a different set of implementation details.

102
00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:55.000
While the structural components and underlying memory mechanics share a translation,

103
00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:59.000
the methodology for styling these elements diverges from traditional XML.

104
00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:04.000
Android's XML style attributes are replaced by the react native style sheet API

105
00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:07.000
and platform specific styling parameters.

106
00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:15.000
Mastering this architectural mapping provides the foundation required before you can successfully style and polish a production-ready application.

